<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">French Equatorial Africa</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Equatorial Africa</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">French Equatorial <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> was a federation of French colonies in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-africa">Central Africa</a>, including present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chad">Chad</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-african-republic">Central African Republic</a>, Congo and Gabon. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wadai-empire">Wadai</a> was incorporated into it after the French conquest, and it formed part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-colonial-empire">French Empire</a> until independence in 1960.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/french-equatorial-africa.jpg" alt="French Equatorial Africa" width="320" height="214" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: ComandanteBruh · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_French_Equatorial_Africa.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>French Equatorial Africa accounted for a little less than an eighth of Africa, across the present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-african-republic">Central African Republic</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chad">Chad</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/republic-of-the-congo">Republic of the Congo</a>, Gabon and most of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cameroon">Cameroon</a>. The greater part of it extended over a granite plateau framed by the Tibesti, Ouadaï and Fertit massifs, with two basins, that of Chad and that of the Congo. The highest point was Mount Emi Koussi, at 3,415 metres, in Tibesti. The climate ran from arid Saharan in the north to humid tropical in the south, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/savannah">savannahs</a> in the centre where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/millet">millet</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peanut">peanuts</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cassava">cassava</a> were grown, and tropical forests in the south that supplied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ebony">ebony</a> and okoumé.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The federation was established in 1910 with four colonial possessions: French Gabon, French Congo, Ubangi-Shari and French Chad. The Governor-General was based in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazzaville">Brazzaville</a> with deputies in each territory. In 1911 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> ceded parts of the territory to German Kamerun after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agadir-crisis">Agadir Crisis</a>, and it was returned after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>'s defeat in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">World War I</a>.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> granted private companies contracts to exploit <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rubber">rubber</a>, and Ubangi-Shari had a concession system like that of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congo-free-state">Congo Free State</a>, with similar atrocities. The writer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andre-gide">André Gide</a> travelled there and was told of mutilations, executions and the burning of children, and his Travels in the Congo, published in 1927, was fiercely critical of the concession companies. The number of victims remains unknown. The French authorities long tolerated indigenous <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a>, but acted against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">slave trade</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> of Dar Kuti in 1908 and 1911, and annexed his territory in 1912.</p><p>Until 1934 the AEF was a federation of colonies, and in that year it became a unitary entity with a single budget. In August 1940 the whole federation except Gabon rallied to the Free French Forces, and Gabon followed after the Battle of Gabon on 12 November 1940. Félix Eboué was installed as Governor-General, and the federation became the strategic centre of Free French activities in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>. In the September 1958 referendum the territories voted to become autonomous within the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-community">French Community</a> and the federation was dissolved. They formed an interim Union of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-african-republic">Central African Republics</a> in 1959 and became fully independent in August 1960.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “French Equatorial Africa” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>West and <b>Equatorial Africa</b> were organised into two large federations headquartered at Dakar and Brazzaville.</blockquote><a href="/empire/french-empire">French Empire · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Wadai was absorbed into the colony of Chad within <b>French Equatorial Africa</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/wadai-empire">Wadai Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
